    historyContent: {
      origins: "Napoleon is a classic English pub game first documented in H.E. Heather's October 1875 edition of The Bazaar. It quickly became England's national five-card game, displacing Loo. The expression 'going Nap' (meaning to risk all on one attempt) derives from this game.",
      originCountries: [
        'United Kingdom',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1875: Earliest rules published by H.E. Heather in The Bazaar',
        '1885: American Hoyle incorrectly claims French origin; more likely European roots',
        '1891: Bids of Wellington, BlÃ¼cher and MisÃ¨re added',
        "1982: Andrew Pennycook notes Nap was 'widely played in pubs'",
        'Present: Still played in parts of Britain',
      ],
      evolution: 'The game evolved from simple five-card bidding to include advanced bids like Wellington and BlÃ¼cher. Variants emerged including Peep Nap, Purchase Nap, and multi-card versions.',
      cultural: 'A quintessential British pub game played for small stakes. The terminology reflects British military history, with bids named after the Duke of Wellington and General BlÃ¼cher who defeated Napoleon at Waterloo.',
