          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win at least the number of tricks bid. The bidder plays against all other players who form a temporary team.',
          gameplay: '1. Bidding: Each player has one chance to bid, starting left of dealer. Must pass or bid higher than previous. Bids in ascending order: Three (3 tricks), Four (4 tricks), Nap (5 tricks), Wellington (5 tricks, higher stakesâ€”can only bid after Nap), BlÃ¼cher (5 tricks, highest stakesâ€”can only bid after Wellington). 2. High bidder leads first trick; suit of this card becomes trumps. 3. Players must follow suit if possible. 4. Highest trump wins trick; if no trumps, highest card of led suit wins. 5. Winner of trick leads next.',
          keyRules: [
            'Five cards each; high bidder names trumps by leading',
            'Must follow suit if possible',
            'One bidding round onlyâ€”pass or overcall',
            'Wellington can only be bid after Nap; BlÃ¼cher only after Wellington',
            'Cards often not shuffled between hands (just cut)',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Be aggressive with your bids if you have a high trump (Ace or King) and at least one other card of the same suit. Since you only have 5 cards, a single strong suit can carry the whole hand. If you aren't the bidder, your only goal is to 'break' the bid by taking just one trick.",
          intermediate: "The 'Overbid' Defense: If you have a solid hand (e.g., King and Queen of a suit) and someone bids 3, you should consider bidding 4 just to prevent them from taking the pot. Even if you fail, you prevent them from certain victory and force them to play defensively next round.",
          advanced: "Counting the BlÃ¼cher: Only bid Wellington or BlÃ¼cher if you hold the Ace and King of your lead suit. If someone else holds the Ace, they will almost certainly 'break' you on the first trick. Wellington is a psychological tool to scare away weaker 'Nap' bidders.",
          tips: [
            'If you are a defender, always lead through the bidder to try and find their weak suit',
            'In Nap, the Ace is your most important cardâ€”never waste it on a trick where someone else has already played a trump unless you have to',
            'With a balanced hand and no strong suit, consider passing rather than bidding low',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
